ckages/evals/tests/test_witnessed_judging.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_writer_prompt_retired.py", "packages/evals/tests/toyhost_plan.py", "packages/registry/tests/unit/registry/test_unsigned_agent_concession_expires.py", "tests/enforcement/_model_literal_exemptions.txt", "tests/enforcement/_public_api_consumers.txt", "tests/enforcement/test_a_tool_states_its_notes_first.py"], "files_oracle": [], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The 'identical statistics' claim for the planning read's cross-run rules is held by no committed test", "recommendation": "Reading the code against 7a98dcfc7, I found the restructured planning_readings equivalent on every rule. Failures read as misses: classified_cases is global, the miss decision is deferred until every run is read, so a classifying run that comes AFTER the failing run still turns the failure into a miss. Grading otherwise: answered_unclassified intersected with the final classified_cases is the old any(...). Boundary dimensions are unioned over raw pages and applied after the loop, and rubric_scores are untouched by the miss and latency rewrites. Rubric scales are last-write-wins in the same run, store and record order. Withholding contended latency is per result, so doing it per page gives the same answer. The judged rows of a miss are taken from the non-miss projection, and that is safe because counted_rubric_scores, counted_score and the dual-axis rows read classify_result, which ignores host_measures['match']. Repeat order is sorted by (case, k, id) per run. The one cross-result input that is now per page, _run_factors and resolve_levers, feeds only ScoreRecord.factors, which _per_case_values never reads. But the commit message's 'checked identical on a history that exercises each rule' is not in the tree. No test of test_power_preflight.py, or of any other detectable_effects or launch_estimate test, sets a 'match' verdict, a candidate failure, a boundary rubric score, a rubric scale or a contended-latency result through this path. Every test history is 30 results or fewer, below RUN_RESULTS_PAGE=200, so the per-page path never runs more than one page per run. Add a test pinning the readings on a history that exercises each rule. It should cover a failing run listed BEFORE the run that classifies the case, a run that grades otherwise, and a boundary dimension scored only in an early page or run. Run it with RUN_RESULTS_PAGE monkeypatched small (it is imported by name in comparisons.py and run_pages.py) so results cross page boundaries. Otherwise the next refactor of this deferred-application logic can break 'exactly the same statistics' with nothing failing.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_power_preflight.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "The tracemalloc test measures peak correctly but never pages within a run, and its stated premise about the payload may not hold on the in-memory store", "recommendation": "_peak calls reset_peak and reads get_traced_memory()[1] minus the current figure, so it measures peak and not just current memory. Each plan() runs once before it is measured, which warms caches. The old code held every hydrated EvalResult plus a whole-history ScoreProjection and judged_rows at once, so it should exceed 4,000 B per added result and the test should fail on it. Two gaps remain. (1) Each run holds 30 results, below RUN_RESULTS_PAGE=200, so an implementation that held a whole run at a time would also pass. The test pins 'flat as history grows', not 'a page at a time'. Monkeypatch the page size small, or add a single long run, if the page bound is meant to be held. (2) The comment says 'Held whole, each added result costs at least its payload'. But InMemoryDocumentStore returns copy.deepcopy rows, which share str objects, and _verbatim_json_object's TypeAdapter likely hands back the same str. So the 16 KB payload may never be allocated per read in this fixture, and the old code would fail on model and projection overhead rather than on payload. Either confirm that the payload is allocated per read (for example, by asserting the old figure the commit cites) or reword the comment so the threshold's rationale matches what is measured.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/test_power_preflight.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The failure-as-miss rule now has two owners: _failures_as_misses and planning_readings each recombine it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The commit pulled out the per-result predicates (_classifies, _answered_unclassified, _as_miss), but the rule that combines them is still written twice. One copy is _failures_as_misses.read in measures.py, which assemble.py uses for every bundle. The other is the deferred loop at the end of planning_readings in comparisons.py. Each copy builds its own classified_cases and its own grading_otherwise (a set of run ids in one, a per-run isdisjoint test in the other) and applies 'case classified AND run not grading otherwise' on its own. planning_readings tracks three interdependent signals: classified_cases, each run's answered_unclassified, and each result's alternate as-miss values. No single model owns them. The docstring promises that 'a repeat's value here and a case's mean in a comparison are one computation', and nothing ties the two copies together. If someone adds a third condition to _failures_as_misses (whose docstring is where the rule is documented), the bundle's comparisons change and the launch estimate's planning readings quietly do not. The commit message says the readings were 'checked identical on a history that exercises each rule', but no test pins that, and no test calls planning_readings with a classifier miss or a boundary dimension. Fix: give the rule one owner in measures.py, for example a small accumulator with observe(run_id, result) and reads_as_miss(run_id, case_id), and rebuild _failures_as_misses on top of it so both readers go through that one object. This also replaces the three new cross-module private imports with one. Add a parity test: planning_readings over a lazily-iterated history that exercises the miss rule, a boundary dimension and a scale override must equal planning_readings over the same history read whole after _failures_as_misses.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/measures.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Descriptions of the planning read still say it holds every result at once, or reads results and then drops them", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Cause: the commit changed two things, holding results whole to walking them a page at a time, and counting results after reading them to counting the runs' keys first, but it edited only the code. Stale sites found: (1) packages/evals/docs/cost-and-budgets.md, the 'What the launch could detect' paragraph, says 'The block holds every earlier run's results at once'. This is a user doc, and it now misstates why the budget applies (it bounds the read's time, not memory). (2) _planning_block's docstring in ops/lenses.py says a read past the budget is refused 'after it, and the results were dropped'. Now no result is read: the keys are counted first. (3) The Unreleased CHANGELOG entry 'a launch's planning block ... refused past the read budget' says 'a read that returned more than the budget is read and dropped'. That entry is unreleased and describes behaviour this commit replaced, and the commit adds no Unreleased entry of its own for the paged read. (4) The class docstring of TestThePlanningReadIsHeldToTheReadBudget in tests/test_power_preflight.py opens 'Every earlier run's results are held at once'. (5) The `returned` parameter of hold_to_read_budget in analysis/reads.py ('How many results the read returned') and its 'holds a set of runs' results whole' framing now receive a key count. Search these files for 'at once', 'whole', 'dropped' and 'returned' near detectable_effects, _planning_block and hold_to_read_budget, and expect more sites. Fix each by deleting the claim or stating it in terms of what is read (keys counted before any result; results walked a page at a time). Add an Unreleased CHANGELOG entry for the paged planning read.", "files": ["packages/evals/docs/cost-and-budgets.md", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/ops/lenses.py", "CHANGELOG.md", "packages/evals/tests/test_power_preflight.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/reads.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "planning_readings docstring names iter_stored_results as its lazy source; the caller uses iter_keyed_results, and a comment narrates history", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The docstring of planning_readings gives ':func:`~threetears.evals.kernel.run_pages.iter_stored_results`' as the example paged walk, but detectable_effects passes iter_keyed_results. Change the reference to iter_keyed_results, or make it relational ('any lazy per-run iterable'). In detectable_effects, the comment ending 'a template's history read whole held every result of every run at once' describes the old behaviour, not the code. Delete that clause. No other edit is wanted.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/ops/lenses.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "_RunRepeats.values is an anonymous 3-tuple of nested dicts, unpacked by position", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Each kept result is stored as ((case, k, id), values, as_miss_values | None) and read back by tuple unpacking that ignores two of the key's parts. A small NamedTuple per kept result (sort_key, case_id, values, as_miss) would give the deferred miss choice named fields. Optional, and best folded into the miss-rule owner above if that is done.", "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The planning read's switch to a paged walk and a key-counted budget is recorded only in a code comment and the commit; two shipped docs still say it holds every earlier result at once", "recommendation": "instance. 9f35752ef makes two decisions. The planning read now walks each run a page at a time and keeps each result's values, not the result. The read budget is now counted off result keys before any result is read, and it now bounds the read's time, not its memory (lenses.py comment: 'Memory is held by the walk below ... the budget holds the read's time'). Neither decision is in a shipped record. packages/evals/docs/cost-and-budgets.md (~line 300) still says 'The block holds every earlier run's results at once'. packages/evals/docs/adopting-a-host.md (~line 791) still says a launch estimate's planning block 'read[s] many runs whole'. CHANGELOG.md Unreleased has no entry for a fix to a production OOM. The consequence is that a host operator who sizes a pod, or decides how many earlier runs to archive, from those docs acts on the old memory model, and nothing tells a consumer that launch estimates no longer hold the whole history. Fix: add an Unreleased CHANGELOG entry and correct the two doc sentences to say what the block holds (a page of results plus each result's per-reading values), and that the budget is counted off the runs' keys before any result is read.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/ops/lenses.py", "packages/evals/docs/cost-and-budgets.md", "packages/evals/docs/adopting-a-host.md", "CHANGELOG.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "What the planning read keeps per result grows with the host's reading count, and the memory test holds it at one reading", "recommendation": "instance. _RunRepeats.values keeps, for every result of every earlier run until all runs are read, a sort key plus a fresh dict[(kind, name)] -> dict[case, float] from _per_case_values. A failure that landed no verdict keeps a second one. That is about one small dict per reading per result, so the cost per result scales with the measures and judged dimensions the host declares. The memory test (TestThePlanningReadHoldsAPageNotTheHistory) uses a profile with a single READING plus the toy measures and measures about 1.2 KB a result. A production host with 20 or more readings plausibly keeps several KB a result. Up to READ_TIER_ROW_BUDGET (50,000 results) and eight concurrent estimates, which is the incident's shape, that can again reach a large fraction of a 2 GB pod. This is an estimate, not a measurement. The collection is bounded by the budget, but nothing pins that bound at the reading count production runs. Either keep the values flat (accumulate straight into `repeats` for non-failures, and defer only the failure-as-miss alternates, which are the only values that depend on the cross-run facts), or parametrize the tracemalloc test with a profile at production's reading count and assert a per-result ceiling that would keep READ_TIER_ROW_BUDGET results x the expected concurrency under the pod limit.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/comparisons.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_power_preflight.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "The budget helper's `returned` and the planning block's docstring still describe a read of results; detectable_effects now passes a key count before anything is read", "recommendation": "instance. Prose, and nothing reads it. The _planning_block docstring in lenses.py still says '(or, a read that returned more than the budget, after it, and the results were dropped)'. hold_to_read_budget's ':param returned: How many results the read returned' and _refuse_past_budget's 'the store returned {returned}' now carry a count of keys from a key-only read. An operator still detects a refusal: one WARNING line 'eval.read_tier lens=detectable_effects ... refused', the block's cannot_estimate text, and ValidationFailedError for a direct call. The number is right, because one key is one result. Delete the 'read and dropped' clause from _planning_block. The helper's wording needs no edit.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/ops/lenses.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/reads.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: no learnings corpus to check against", "recommendation": "none. `prawduct-hook learnings-files --for-diff` printed no files, and .claude/rules/learnings/ does not exist in this worktree, so no rule could be escalated or cited. The project's CLAUDE.md rules were checked directly against the change: under docs/exceptions.md a mid-stream StorageError from load_eval_results reaches the caller (it propagates out of iter_keyed_results, planning_readings and detectable_effects, and _planning_block re-raises anything that is not a budget refusal). No broad handler was added. No private name is bound outside its module: _classifies, _as_miss and _answered_unclassified are imported by comparisons.py from the sibling bundle.measures module, the same pattern the existing _failures_as_misses import already used.", "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog Reconciliation unavailable", "recommendation": "none. Backlog reconciliation unavailable: `prawduct-hook backlog cache-query --repo pacepace/3tears affecting ...` exited 6 with 'the backlog cache has not been built yet'. Run `prawduct-hook backlog sync --repo pacepace/3tears`. The commit cites #996 (the store computing the planning read) as still open, and the change does not resolve it.", "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate"